  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on comparing the maximum probability of a single outcome between the two lotteries. Lottery A has a maximum probability of 100%, Lottery B of 66%, so the reason would lead to choosing A. The individual did consider the high probability of winning in Lottery B (99% combined), but also explicitly contrasted it with the 1% chance of nothing and ultimately valued the certainty in A. While they did not explicitly state they were comparing 'maximum probabilities of single outcomes', they did emphasize the 100% certainty of A versus the small chance of zero in B. This aligns closely with the core idea of the reason—favoring the option with the highest single outcome probability—even if the reasoning was framed more in terms of risk aversion and certainty than in abstract probabilistic comparison. The individual’s focus on the 1% risk and the value of 100% certainty suggests they were effectively responding to the maximum probability criterion, even if not articulated in technical terms. Therefore, the decision reason appears to be consistent with the individual’s deliberation.",
